INT. FUNICULAR CAR - NIGHT

A little wooden car climbs a steep rail through pine forest toward a sleeping hotel. Brass lamps, velvet benches, a locked steel case strapped to the floor.

MIRABEL STROUD, 40s, sleek in a conductor's uniform that does not quite fit, stands at the brake lever. JUNIOR OKAFOR, 20s, in porter's livery, perches on the case with a thermos. LUCAS PIERRE, 50s, a quiet man in a traveling coat, sits at the far end with a newspaper.

JUNIOR
Smooth ride, Mirabel. Real smooth.

MIRABEL
Don't jinx it.

LUCAS
(not looking up)
Nothing to jinx. Two minutes to the top, one case, three honest people.

JUNIOR
Four if you count the case.

MIRABEL
Junior, the cable.

JUNIOR
What about it?

Mirabel's eyes go to the cable humming in its groove. Junior unscrews the thermos, takes a sip, offers it to Lucas, who shakes his head, folds the newspaper in half, and tucks it under his arm, while Mirabel slides one hand off the brake lever and into her jacket, and the car creaks past a lantern marking the halfway pole.

LUCAS
Tell me, Mirabel. Who's at the top?

MIRABEL
Night clerk. Maybe a guest or two.

JUNIOR
The clerk's asleep.

LUCAS
How would you know?

JUNIOR
I did the schedule.

MIRABEL
You did the schedule?

JUNIOR
I did a schedule. Not the schedule.

LUCAS
(softly)
Interesting.

Junior stands. The thermos slips from his hand and rolls to Lucas's shoe. Lucas stops it with a toe, picks it up, unscrews the cap, and tips the contents onto the floor. It is not coffee. It is a thin pool of clear oil that spreads toward the case.

MIRABEL
That's brake fluid.

JUNIOR
It's not what it looks like.

LUCAS
It's exactly what it looks like. Mirabel, stop the car.

MIRABEL
If I stop here, we slide.

LUCAS
Then slide gently.

Mirabel draws a pistol from her jacket and levels it at Junior. Lucas stands, opens his traveling coat, and shows a badge.

LUCAS (CONT'D)
Funicular Authority, Inspector Pierre. Which of you wants to explain the oil?
